## Third-party connector brand icons

Shipped connector manifests in `packages/polyfill-connectors/manifests/` each
declare a small brand glyph (`brand.icon`, under `manifests/icons/*.svg`) to
identify the third-party service the connector connects to (e.g. Amazon,
GitHub, Spotify). All product names, trademarks, and registered trademarks
referenced or depicted by these icons are the property of their respective
owners. These icons are used by the PDP-Connect project strictly to identify
the products and companies a connector integrates with; their use implies no
sponsorship, affiliation, or endorsement by the mark holder. This mirrors the
posture used by home-assistant/brands: identification only, no license to
the trademarks themselves.

Where an icon's SVG markup is drawn from `simple-icons`
(https://simpleicons.org), it is released under CC0 1.0, but per that
project's own LICENSE.md the CC0 waiver explicitly does not extend to
trademark or patent rights, and simple-icons maintains a brand-owner
takedown channel. See
https://github.com/simple-icons/simple-icons/blob/master/DISCLAIMER.md.

Where an icon's SVG markup is drawn from the `logos` collection published via
`@iconify/json` (https://github.com/gilbarbara/logos), it is released under
CC0 1.0. As with simple-icons, CC0 waives copyright in the markup only; it
does not and cannot waive any trademark rights the depicted brand owner
holds. This source is used only where no legitimately available mark exists
in simple-icons and the source's own artwork is genuine brand art, not an
invented or hand-drawn substitute.

A connector manifest with no legitimately available brand mark should not
carry an invented or unofficial logo. Consumers of this package's
`connector-index.json` render a deterministic monogram (the connector's
initial(s) and a color derived from its name) as the correct default for
that case — this is a normal, first-class fallback, not an error state.
